# 53. Common Misunderstandings

## “Read replicas scale everything.”

False. They scale reads. Writes still pass through the master.

## “Replicas are always current.”

False. Asynchronous replication creates lag.

## “Read-your-writes means everyone sees the latest value.”

False. It guarantees immediate visibility to the writer.

## “Monotonic reads always show the latest data.”

False. They prevent moving backward but may still show stale state.

## “Master-master automatically improves correctness.”

False. It improves write locality and availability but introduces conflicts.

## “The newest timestamp is always correct.”

False. Last-writer-wins may destroy valid intent.

## “A shard key only needs to balance row count.”

False. It must also balance traffic and support common queries.

## “Consistent hashing guarantees equal traffic.”

False. Virtual nodes improve balance, but hot keys and unequal hardware still matter.

## “Federation removes integration complexity.”

False. It moves integration from SQL joins to services and pipelines.

## “Denormalization is always bad design.”

False. It is a deliberate read-performance trade-off.

