# 54. Red Flags and Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- Adding replicas without changing read routing
- Sending every `SELECT` to a replica
- Serving recent writers from lagging replicas
- Using synchronous replication for every table
- Choosing master-master only to avoid failover design
- Using last-writer-wins for money or inventory
- Allowing both sides of a partition to make conflicting ownership decisions
- Sharding before indexing, caching, or query optimization
- Using sequential shard keys for current-time writes without hotspot planning
- Using hash sharding when important queries are ranges
- Assuming equal row count means equal load
- Running global analytics across production shards
- Using modulo hashing in frequently changing clusters
- Using too few virtual nodes
- Assuming consistent hashing solves hot keys
- Creating databases without meaningful domain boundaries
- Using polyglot persistence without operational expertise
- Denormalizing without propagation and repair
- Building materialized views without freshness monitoring

