# 60. Top 15 Points to Remember

1. Master-slave replication primarily scales reads.
2. All writes still pass through the master.
3. Asynchronous replication creates expected lag.
4. Read-your-writes protects the writer’s experience.
5. Monotonic reads prevent users from moving backward.
6. Synchronous replication should be selective.
7. Master-master introduces unavoidable conflict decisions.
8. Last-writer-wins may silently discard valid intent.
9. Sharding divides data and scales writes and storage.
10. Shard keys must balance traffic, not only rows.
11. Range sharding favors ordered access.
12. Hash sharding favors even distribution.
13. Consistent hashing limits movement during node changes.
14. Virtual nodes improve balance, but hot keys need separate handling.
15. Federation separates domains, while denormalization accelerates reads.

